00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.320
From a performance perspective,
one of the most important

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.110
things to consider while
designing your Bigtable schema

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.360
is your index design,
which is something

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.090
that our friends at Urban
Fitness Forge, makers

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.510
of Fitness Tracker products,
ran into headlong recently.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.940
UFF's usage pattern
for their data

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.150
dominantly queried lat-long
values to find specific sensors

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.770
within an area for analysis.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.290
Or rather, given how
often they ran this query,

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.610
it was way too slow
for their needs.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.090
Their initial schema
design used a row key

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.950
comprised of lat-long values.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.960
And as it turned out,
this simple setup

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.430
was exactly the problem.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.740
The reason for this is two
things working together.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.350
Firstly, Bigtable doesn't
have explicit support

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.354
for secondary indexes.

00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:53.020
So if you want to
find a group of people

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.020
with the same first
and last name,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.160
then you can get a very
fast first name search,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.530
but you have to do a
secondary search in a less

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.970
than performant fashion.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.180
Secondly, entries in a tablet
are sorted lexicographically

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.970
by their index, meaning that
entries with similar keys

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.210
will be closer to each
other in the tablet space.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.570
Now, since the dominant
part of UFF's key

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.880
was the latitude
value, other cities

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.490
which shared the same latitude
value were listed closely

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.760
in the table, meaning that their
secondary scan for a longitude

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.640
value was walking through
loads more data, and of course

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.500
was less performant as a result.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.210
This highlights an
important aspect

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.980
of Bigtable schema design.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.370
The wrong key design could spell
disaster for your performance.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.100
For Urban Fitness Forge,
that meant creating an index

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.960
such that entries that are close
to each other in physical space

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.080
were sorted close to
each other in tablespace

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.070
to reduce scan size.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.570
So instead of using a lat-long
value which produced a Raster

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.940
style order index
like this one, they

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.180
were able to use a space-filling
curve which organized indexes

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.490
in physical space like this.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.010
The result is a modified
index for entries

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.160
which use a space-filling curve
integrated with a timestamp

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.130
value to create a single
integer-based index.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.660
For Urban Fitness
Forge, this meant

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.670
queries which looked
for lat-long data,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.759
had reduced table scans,
resulting in a huge perf

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:10.560
improvement.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.660
Now obviously, using
a space-filling curve

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.580
is not the right solution
for every problem,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.070
but it does offer a good
reminder-- to get the best

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.510
performance out
of Cloud Bigtable,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.970
it's essential to think
carefully about how

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.342
you compose your row key.

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:25.550
If you're interested to know
more about this problem,

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.560
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.620
And don't forget to
subscribe to our YouTube

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.979
channel for more great Cloud
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:34.520
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.710
every millisecond counts.

